<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Marcel Proust</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1871">1871</a>–<a href="/years/1922">1922</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Marcel Proust was a French novelist, literary critic and essayist, author of À la recherche du temps perdu (In Search of Lost Time), published in seven volumes between 1913 and 1927 and totalling around 1.25 million words. Born in the Auteuil quarter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> to a wealthy bourgeois family, he suffered severe asthma from a young age and moved in the salons of Parisian high society, which gave material for the novel. He began it in 1908; the later volumes were revised and published after his death by his brother Robert. The work helped pioneer the stream of consciousness technique. He died of pneumonia in 1922, aged 51.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/marcel-proust.jpg" alt="Marcel Proust" width="330" height="411" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Otto Wegener · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Otto_Wegener_Proust_vers_1895_bis.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Proust was born on 10 July 1871, two months after the Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankfurt">Frankfurt</a> ended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franco-prussian-war">Franco-Prussian War</a>, at the house of his great-uncle in Auteuil. His father, Adrien Proust, was a pathologist and epidemiologist who studied cholera. His mother, Jeanne Clémence Weil, came from a wealthy German-Jewish family from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alsace">Alsace</a>. He was baptised a Catholic but never formally practised the faith.</p><p>He spent long holidays in the village of Illiers, which together with his great-uncle's house became the model for the fictional Combray; Illiers was renamed Illiers-Combray in 1971. He entered the Lycée Condorcet in 1882, where his schooling was disrupted by illness. His father died in November 1903 and his mother in September 1905.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Les plaisirs et les jours, a collection of early pieces with a foreword by Anatole France, appeared in 1896 and sold poorly because of its high price. He then began an unfinished novel, published in 1952 as Jean Santeuil, and abandoned it by 1899.</p><p>From 1895 he read Thomas Carlyle, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ralph-waldo-emerson">Ralph Waldo Emerson</a> and John Ruskin. With help from his mother and from Marie Nordlinger he translated Ruskin's The Bible of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amiens">Amiens</a>, published in 1904, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sesame">Sesame</a> and Lilies, published in 1906. Asked about his English, he said: &quot;I don't claim to know English; I claim to know Ruskin&quot;. In 1908 he wrote pastiches of other writers and sketched essays that became Contre Sainte-Beuve; from such fragments a novel took shape, and by 1910 he was at work on À la recherche du temps perdu.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>The first volume was refused by Gallimard on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andre-gide">André Gide</a>'s advice, and Gide later called it one of the most serious mistakes of his life. It was published at the author's expense by Grasset. C. K. Scott Moncrieff's English translation, Remembrance of Things Past, appeared between 1922 and 1931, and later revisers changed the title to In Search of Lost Time.</p><p>Proust spent his last three years mostly confined to his bedroom at 44 rue Hamelin, sleeping by day and writing at night. He died of pneumonia and a pulmonary abscess and was buried in the Père Lachaise Cemetery.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Marcel Proust” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Writers as diverse as Henry Fielding and Naguib Mahfouz allude to the Nights by name, and among those it influenced are Jorge Luis Borges, Italo Calvino, Angela Carter, Gustave Flaubert, Goethe, Victor Hugo, <b>Marcel Proust</b>, Alexander Pushkin, Salman Rushdie, Leo Tolstoy and W. B. Yeats.</blockquote><a href="/work/one-thousand-and-one-nights">One Thousand and One Nights · Legacy</a></li></ul></section>
